**Security in the cloud** is your half. What it includes depends on the services you choose, but the pattern is the same: you control how you configure and use the service, so you secure that configuration and what you put into it.

Typical customer jobs:
- manage your **data**, including encryption options (encryption means scrambling data so only someone with the right key can read it; a later chapter teaches it properly);
- decide **who may do what** with IAM policies, applying the principle of least privilege you met in the IAM chapter;
- classify your assets, meaning decide which data is more sensitive than other data;
- configure your **security groups**, the firewall in front of your resources.

AWS says that for abstracted services such as Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB, customers are responsible for managing their data (including encryption options), classifying their assets, and using IAM tools to apply the appropriate permissions.

With Amazon EC2, AWS manages the hardware layers: server maintenance, hardware lifecycle, and power, network and cooling. The virtual machine itself is yours.

AWS's EC2 security guide lists what your responsibility includes:
- controlling network access to your instances, for example by configuring your VPC and security groups;
- managing the credentials used to connect to your instances;
- managing the guest operating system and the software deployed on it, including updates and security patches;
- configuring the IAM roles attached to the instance and the permissions of those roles.

Figure 10-2 below shows this as a grid. Each row is a layer, each cell says who looks after it. Only the EC2 column is filled in so far.

Basic Support is included for all AWS customers. It offers assistance for account and billing questions and for service quota increases. All customers automatically have 24x7 access to:

- one-on-one responses to account and billing questions;
- support forums;
- service health checks;
- documentation, technical papers and best practice guides.

AWS's plan page sums this up as customer service and communities: 24/7 access to customer service, documentation, whitepapers and AWS re:Post. It also gives core checks of AWS Trusted Advisor, which the later lessons come back to.

AWS describes Business Support+ as delivering AI-powered assistance that understands the context of your operations, with 24/7 access to AWS experts. Compared with Basic Support, it adds:

- real-time, contextual answers from generative AI;
- 24x7 phone, web and chat access to Cloud Support Engineers;
- unlimited support cases, which any user with permission can open;
- the full set of AWS Trusted Advisor checks and the AWS Support API, which automates case management;
- help with EC2 operating systems and with popular third-party software on AWS.

Figure 19-1 below puts Basic Support and Business Support+ side by side.

Enterprise Support has all the benefits of Business Support+, plus:

- a designated Technical Account Manager (TAM);
- a response of up to 15 minutes for production-critical cases;
- access to AWS Security Incident Response, which helps you prepare for, respond to and recover from incidents such as account takeovers, data breaches and ransomware;
- strategic business reviews, and AWS Countdown event management for critical business events.

AWS recommends it for business-critical workloads across organizations that want expert guidance. The TAM leads Well-Architected Reviews and gives guidance across the pillars you met earlier.

AWS Unified Operations is for mission-critical workloads that need enhanced resilience and application-specific expertise. It brings:

- a designated TAM and Domain Specialist Engineers for your use cases;
- 24/7 workload monitoring, and a response of 5 minutes from an Incident Management Engineer for critical incidents;
- critical workload reviews, application architecture guidance, personalized runbooks (step-by-step guides for handling known problems) and operating procedures;
- infrastructure event management for planned events.

