Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and recommends actions when there are chances to save money, improve availability and performance, or close security gaps. Its cost optimization checks look for unused and underused resources and for savings you can make by committing to Reserved Instances or Savings Plans.

Examples of cost optimization checks:

- idle load balancers;
- low utilization Amazon EC2 instances;
- unassociated Elastic IP addresses;
- underutilized Amazon EBS volumes;
- idle Amazon RDS DB instances;
- Savings Plans purchase recommendations for compute;
- an AWS account that is not part of AWS Organizations, which ties back to consolidated billing.

The AWS Trust and Safety team plays a crucial role in addressing abuse of AWS services. Abuse is any use of AWS services in a manner that violates the AWS Service Terms and the AWS Acceptable Use Policy. Types of abuse include copyright violations, intrusion attempts, email spam, DDoS and phishing.

Typical cases that the team handles:

- objectionable web content hosted on an AWS resource;
- abusive email or spam sent from an AWS resource;
- network activity from an AWS resource that causes problematic traffic, such as an intrusion attempt, a DDoS attack or port scanning;
- copyright complaints under the Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA).

To become a Partner, a company signs in to the console and registers in AWS Partner Central, the place where Partners manage their engagement with AWS. Partner Central gives access to benefits, programs and APN membership.

What AWS offers Partners, from its own Partner pages:

- certifications and training, digital and classroom;
- events: webinars, virtual workshops and in-person events;
- a way to sell or resell software and services in AWS Marketplace, and to co-sell with AWS;
- programs, funding and tools for building and growing their business.

The exam guide also names partner volume discounts as one example of a benefit. Check AWS Partner Central for what is current.
